<%
/*
  Drop inside any public <form> that creates a record or sends mail. Pairs with
  captcha.service.js's requireHuman() on the matching route — one without the
  other is either a check nobody can pass or a form nobody checks.

  Two layers, deliberately:

  The honeypot needs no keys and no third party, so it works today. Most abuse
  is a script filling every field it can find, and a field no human ever sees
  catches that outright. It is inside the form so the existing
  FormData -> Object.fromEntries submit path picks it up with no JS changes.

  Turnstile renders only once TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY is set, and writes its result
  into a hidden input named captchaToken — the name the server reads — via
  data-response-field-name, so it too rides along with the existing submit.
*/
%>
<div class="hidden" aria-hidden="true">
  <%
    /*
      Named "website" rather than something like "honeypot": a bot fills what
      looks fillable, so a plausible name catches far more than an honest one.
      tabindex="-1" keeps it out of keyboard navigation, and autocomplete="off"
      stops a browser helpfully filling it on a real person's behalf — which
      would lock them out of the form they are trying to use.
    */
  %>
  <label>Website
    <input type="text" name="website" tabindex="-1" autocomplete="off" value="">
  </label>
</div>

<% if (locals.turnstileSiteKey) { %>
  <div class="cf-turnstile my-3"
       data-sitekey="<%= turnstileSiteKey %>"
       data-response-field-name="captchaToken"
       data-theme="auto"
       data-appearance="interaction-only"></div>
<% } %>
